apraxic gait); (4) **Psychomotor slowing**; (5) **Pseudobulbar affect** (involuntary laughing or crying); (6) **Urinary urgency/incontinence**. MRI shows extensive **confluent periventricular and deep white matter hyperintensities** on FLAIR/T2-weighted sequences. The primary risk factor is **chronic hypertension**. Management focuses on aggressive BP control and vascular risk factor modification. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
