ed, or apraxic gait); (4) **Psychomotor slowing**; (5) **Pseudobulbar affect** (involuntary laughing or crying); (6) **Urinary urgency/incontinence**. MRI shows extensive **confluent periventricular and deep white matter hyperintensities** on FLAIR/T2-weighted sequences. The primary risk factor is **chronic hypertension**. Management focuses on aggressive BP control and vascular risk factor modification. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old male with a long history of poorly controlled hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents with a gradual decline in processing speed, executive function, and gait over the past 3 years. The patient has become increasingly apathetic and emotionally labile. There is no clear stepwise pattern. Examination shows a shuffling, wide-based gait, pseudobulbar affect, and frontal release signs. Brain MRI reveals **extensive confluent periventricular and deep white matter hyperintensities** with relative preservation of cortical gray matter. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus \u2014 defined by enlarged ventricles compressing periventricular white matter with the triad of gait apraxia, dementia, and urinary incontinence", "Subcortical ischemic vascular dementia (Binswanger''''s disease) \u2014 caused by chronic small vessel disease leading to diffuse white matter injury, executive dysfunction, gait disturbance, and emotional lability", "Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 characterized by progressive cortical atrophy with prominent hippocampal involvement and early memory loss", "Frontotemporal dementia \u2014 characterized by frontal and anterior temporal lobe atrophy with prominent behavioral changes and personality deterioration", "Multiple sclerosis \u2014 characterized by periventricular demyelinating plaques with relapsing-remitting neurological deficits in young adults"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Subcortical ischemic vascular dementia (Binswanger''''s disease)** is a subtype of vascular dementia caused by **chronic small vessel disease** (lipohyalinosis, arteriosclerosis) leading to **diffuse white matter injury** (leukoaraiosis). Key features include: (1) **Gradual rather than stepwise decline** (unlike multi-infarct dementia); (2) Prominent **executive dysfunction** (planning, organizing, multitasking) with relatively preserved episodic memory early on; (3) **Gait disturbance** (shuffling, wide-based, or apraxic gait); (4) **Psychomotor slowing**; (5) **Pseudobulbar affect** (involuntary laughing or crying); (6) **Urinary urgency/incontinence**. MRI shows extensive **confluent periventricular and deep white matter hyperintensities** on FLAIR/T2-weighted sequences. The primary risk factor is **chronic hypertension**. Management focuses on aggressive BP control and vascular risk factor modification. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old woman presents with cognitive decline over the past 2 years. The family asks how to differentiate Alzheimer''''s disease from vascular dementia. Which of the following clinical features is MOST suggestive of **vascular dementia** rather than Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Early and prominent personality changes with disinhibition, loss of empathy, and hyperorality, with relative preservation of memory", "Insidious onset with slowly progressive memory impairment as the earliest and most prominent symptom, with no focal neurological signs and bilateral hippocampal atrophy on MRI", "Fluctuating cognition with pronounced variations in attention, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism", "Rapid cognitive decline over weeks with startle myoclonus, cerebellar ataxia, and periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG", "Stepwise deterioration in cognition temporally related to cerebrovascular events, prominent executive dysfunction early in the course, focal neurological signs on examination, and evidence of cerebrovascular disease on neuroimaging"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Differentiating **vascular dementia (VaD)** from **Alzheimer''''s disease (AD)** is a high-yield clinical skill. Key distinguishing features of **VaD** include: (1) **Stepwise or fluctuating decline** temporally related to cerebrovascular events (vs. AD''''s insidious, gradual progression); (2) **Early executive dysfunction** — impaired planning, judgment, and organization (vs. AD''''s early episodic memory loss); (3) **Focal neurological signs** — hemiparesis, visual field cuts, gait abnormalities, pseudobulbar palsy (vs. AD which typically has no focal signs until late); (4) **Vascular risk factors** — hypertension, diabetes, atrial fibrillation, smoking; (5) **Neuroimaging** — multiple infarcts, white matter hyperintensities, strategic infarcts (vs. AD''''s hippocampal/temporoparietal atrophy). The Hachinski Ischemic Score can help differentiate: score ≥7 suggests VaD, ≤4 suggests AD. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old man presents with cognitive decline over the past 2 years. The family asks how to differentiate Alzheimer''''s disease from vascular dementia. Which of the following clinical features is MOST suggestive of **vascular dementia** rather than Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Rapid cognitive decline over weeks with startle myoclonus, cerebellar ataxia, and periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG", "Stepwise deterioration in cognition temporally related to cerebrovascular events, prominent executive dysfunction early in the course, focal neurological signs on examination, and evidence of cerebrovascular disease on neuroimaging", "Early and prominent personality changes with disinhibition, loss of empathy, and hyperorality, with relative preservation of memory", "Insidious onset with slowly progressive memory impairment as the earliest and most prominent symptom, with no focal neurological signs and bilateral hippocampal atrophy on MRI", "Fluctuating cognition with pronounced variations in attention, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Differentiating **vascular dementia (VaD)** from **Alzheimer''''s disease (AD)** is a high-yield clinical skill. Key distinguishing features of **VaD** include: (1) **Stepwise or fluctuating decline** temporally related to cerebrovascular events (vs. AD''''s insidious, gradual progression); (2) **Early executive dysfunction** — impaired planning, judgment, and organization (vs. AD''''s early episodic memory loss); (3) **Focal neurological signs** — hemiparesis, visual field cuts, gait abnormalities, pseudobulbar palsy (vs. AD which typically has no focal signs until late); (4) **Vascular risk factors** — hypertension, diabetes, atrial fibrillation, smoking; (5) **Neuroimaging** — multiple infarcts, white matter hyperintensities, strategic infarcts (vs. AD''''s hippocampal/temporoparietal atrophy). The Hachinski Ischemic Score can help differentiate: score ≥7 suggests VaD, ≤4 suggests AD. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
