    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female presents with cognitive decline over the past 4 years. The family asks how to differentiate Alzheimer''''s disease from vascular dementia. Which of the following clinical features is MOST suggestive of **vascular dementia** rather than Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Early and prominent personality changes with disinhibition, loss of empathy, and hyperorality, with relative preservation of memory", "Insidious onset with slowly progressive memory impairment as the earliest and most prominent symptom, with no focal neurological signs and bilateral hippocampal atrophy on MRI", "Stepwise deterioration in cognition temporally related to cerebrovascular events, prominent executive dysfunction early in the course, focal neurological signs on examination, and evidence of cerebrovascular disease on neuroimaging", "Rapid cognitive decline over weeks with startle myoclonus, cerebellar ataxia, and periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG", "Fluctuating cognition with pronounced variations in attention, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Differentiating **vascular dementia (VaD)** from **Alzheimer''''s disease (AD)** is a high-yield clinical skill. Key distinguishing features of **VaD** include: (1) **Stepwise or fluctuating decline** temporally related to cerebrovascular events (vs. AD''''s insidious, gradual progression); (2) **Early executive dysfunction** — impaired planning, judgment, and organization (vs. AD''''s early episodic memory loss); (3) **Focal neurological signs** — hemiparesis, visual field cuts, gait abnormalities, pseudobulbar palsy (vs. AD which typically has no focal signs until late); (4) **Vascular risk factors** — hypertension, diabetes, atrial fibrillation, smoking; (5) **Neuroimaging** — multiple infarcts, white matter hyperintensities, strategic infarcts (vs. AD''''s hippocampal/temporoparietal atrophy). The Hachinski Ischemic Score can help differentiate: score ≥7 suggests VaD, ≤4 suggests AD. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
