ggests VaD, ≤4 suggests AD. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old man presents with cognitive decline over the past 3 years. The family asks how to differentiate Alzheimer''''s disease from vascular dementia. Which of the following clinical features is MOST suggestive of **vascular dementia** rather than Alzheimer''''s disease?', '["Early and prominent personality changes with disinhibition, loss of empathy, and hyperorality, with relative preservation of memory", "Rapid cognitive decline over weeks with startle myoclonus, cerebellar ataxia, and periodic sharp wave complexes on EEG", "Fluctuating cognition with pronounced variations in attention, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism", "Insidious onset with slowly progressive memory impairment as the earliest and most prominent symptom, with no focal neurological signs and bilateral hippocampal atrophy on MRI", "Stepwise deterioration in cognition temporally related to cerebrovascular events, prominent executive dysfunction early in the course, focal neurological signs on examination, and evidence of cerebrovascular disease on neuroimaging"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Differentiating **vascular dementia (VaD)** from **Alzheimer''''s disease (AD)** is a high-yield clinical skill. Key distinguishing features of **VaD** include: (1) **Stepwise or fluctuating decline** temporally related to cerebrovascular events (vs. AD''''s insidious, gradual progression); (2) **Early executive dysfunction** — impaired planning, judgment, and organization (vs. AD''''s early episodic memory loss); (3) **Focal neurological signs** — hemiparesis, visual field cuts, gait abnormalities, pseudobulbar palsy (vs. AD which typically has no focal signs until late); (4) **Vascular risk factors** — hypertension, diabetes, atrial fibrillation, smoking; (5) **Neuroimaging** — multiple infarcts, white matter hyperintensities, strategic infarcts (vs. AD''''s hippocampal/temporoparietal atrophy). The Hachinski Ischemic Score can help differentiate: score ≥7 suggests VaD, ≤4 suggests AD. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 79-year-old female with a history of hypertension, diabetes, prior stroke, and a 2-year history of gradually progressive memory loss is evaluated for worsening cognition. Cognitive testing shows deficits in both **episodic memory** and **executive function**. Brain MRI reveals bilateral **hippocampal atrophy** AND **multiple periventricular white matter hyperintensities with old lacunar infarcts**. CSF biomarkers show decreased Aβ42 and elevated p-tau. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and management approach?', '["Mixed dementia (coexisting Alzheimer''''s disease and vascular dementia); manage with both cardiovascular risk factor modification AND cholinesterase inhibitor therapy, as the AD component may respond to pharmacotherapy", "Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus \u2014 the combined imaging findings indicate obstructive hydrocephalus requiring VP shunt placement", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease \u2014 the combination of memory and executive deficits with white matter changes suggests prion disease", "Pure vascular dementia \u2014 the hippocampal atrophy is a normal finding in elderly patients; manage with cardiovascular risk factor modification alone", "Pure Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 the white matter changes are age-related and clinically insignificant; manage with cholinesterase inhibitors alone"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Mixed dementia** — the coexistence of **Alzheimer''''s disease** and **cerebrovascular disease** — is the most common pathological finding in autopsy studies of elderly patients with dementia, accounting for up to 50% of cases in patients >80 years old. This patient demonstrates features of BOTH: AD features (gradually progressive memory loss, hippocampal atrophy, decreased CSF Aβ42 and elevated p-tau) AND VaD features (vascular risk factors, prior stroke, white matter hyperintensities, lacunar infarcts). Management should address BOTH pathologies: (1) **Cardiovascular risk factor modification** (BP control, statins, glycemic optimization, antiplatelets/anticoagulation as indicated) for the vascular component; (2) **Cholinesterase inhibitors ± memantine** for the Alzheimer''''s component. Treating only one etiology is insufficient. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
