    (v_topic_id, 'An 86-year-old woman with a history of hypertension, diabetes, prior stroke, and a 2-year history of gradually progressive memory loss is evaluated for worsening cognition. Cognitive testing shows deficits in both **episodic memory** and **executive function**. Brain MRI reveals bilateral **hippocampal atrophy** AND **multiple periventricular white matter hyperintensities with old lacunar infarcts**. CSF biomarkers show decreased Aβ42 and elevated p-tau. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and management approach?', '["Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus \u2014 the combined imaging findings indicate obstructive hydrocephalus requiring VP shunt placement", "Pure vascular dementia \u2014 the hippocampal atrophy is a normal finding in elderly patients; manage with cardiovascular risk factor modification alone", "Pure Alzheimer''''s disease \u2014 the white matter changes are age-related and clinically insignificant; manage with cholinesterase inhibitors alone", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease \u2014 the combination of memory and executive deficits with white matter changes suggests prion disease", "Mixed dementia (coexisting Alzheimer''''s disease and vascular dementia); manage with both cardiovascular risk factor modification AND cholinesterase inhibitor therapy, as the AD component may respond to pharmacotherapy"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Mixed dementia** — the coexistence of **Alzheimer''''s disease** and **cerebrovascular disease** — is the most common pathological finding in autopsy studies of elderly patients with dementia, accounting for up to 50% of cases in patients >80 years old. This patient demonstrates features of BOTH: AD features (gradually progressive memory loss, hippocampal atrophy, decreased CSF Aβ42 and elevated p-tau) AND VaD features (vascular risk factors, prior stroke, white matter hyperintensities, lacunar infarcts). Management should address BOTH pathologies: (1) **Cardiovascular risk factor modification** (BP control, statins, glycemic optimization, antiplatelets/anticoagulation as indicated) for the vascular component; (2) **Cholinesterase inhibitors ± memantine** for the Alzheimer''''s component. Treating only one etiology is insufficient. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 44-year-old man with a history of recurrent migraines with aura since age 25 and a prior ischemic stroke at age 38 presents with progressive executive dysfunction, apathy, and gait unsteadiness over the past 4 years. Family history reveals that the patient''''s mother had recurrent strokes beginning at age 40 and developed dementia by age 50. Brain MRI shows **extensive white matter hyperintensities with characteristic involvement of the anterior temporal poles and external capsules**, with no significant atherosclerotic disease. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Primary CNS vasculitis \u2014 inflammatory destruction of cerebral blood vessel walls requiring brain biopsy for diagnosis", "Fabry disease \u2014 an X-linked lysosomal storage disease causing stroke in young adults due to alpha-galactosidase A deficiency", "CADASIL (Cerebral Autosomal Dominant Arteriopathy with Subcortical Infarcts and Leukoencephalopathy) \u2014 caused by mutations in the NOTCH3 gene on chromosome 19, leading to non-atherosclerotic small vessel disease", "Moyamoya disease \u2014 progressive stenosis of the internal carotid arteries with collateral vessel formation on cerebral angiography", "Multiple sclerosis \u2014 a demyelinating disease characterized by periventricular plaques, optic neuritis, and relapsing-remitting course"]'::jsonb, 2, '**CADASIL** (Cerebral Autosomal Dominant Arteriopathy with Subcortical Infarcts and Leukoencephalopathy) is the most common hereditary cause of vascular dementia. Key features include: (1) **Autosomal dominant** inheritance — mutations in the **NOTCH3 gene** (chromosome 19p13) causing non-atherosclerotic degeneration of small vessel smooth muscle cells; (2) **Migraine with aura** — often the earliest symptom, beginning in the 20s-30s; (3) **Recurrent ischemic strokes** — typically lacunar, beginning in the 30s-50s; (4) **Progressive cognitive decline** — subcortical vascular dementia with executive dysfunction; (5) **Psychiatric symptoms** — depression, apathy, pseudobulbar affect. MRI shows **extensive white matter hyperintensities** with **characteristic involvement of the anterior temporal poles and external capsules** (a finding highly suggestive of CADASIL). Diagnosis is confirmed by NOTCH3 gene testing or skin biopsy showing granular osmiophilic material (GOM) in vessel walls. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
