    (v_topic_id, 'A 46-year-old female with a history of recurrent migraines with aura since age 25 and a prior ischemic stroke at age 38 presents with progressive executive dysfunction, apathy, and gait unsteadiness over the past 2 years. Family history reveals that the patient''''s mother had recurrent strokes beginning at age 40 and developed dementia by age 50. Brain MRI shows **extensive white matter hyperintensities with characteristic involvement of the anterior temporal poles and external capsules**, with no significant atherosclerotic disease. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["CADASIL (Cerebral Autosomal Dominant Arteriopathy with Subcortical Infarcts and Leukoencephalopathy) \u2014 caused by mutations in the NOTCH3 gene on chromosome 19, leading to non-atherosclerotic small vessel disease", "Multiple sclerosis \u2014 a demyelinating disease characterized by periventricular plaques, optic neuritis, and relapsing-remitting course", "Primary CNS vasculitis \u2014 inflammatory destruction of cerebral blood vessel walls requiring brain biopsy for diagnosis", "Fabry disease \u2014 an X-linked lysosomal storage disease causing stroke in young adults due to alpha-galactosidase A deficiency", "Moyamoya disease \u2014 progressive stenosis of the internal carotid arteries with collateral vessel formation on cerebral angiography"]'::jsonb, 0, '**CADASIL** (Cerebral Autosomal Dominant Arteriopathy with Subcortical Infarcts and Leukoencephalopathy) is the most common hereditary cause of vascular dementia. Key features include: (1) **Autosomal dominant** inheritance — mutations in the **NOTCH3 gene** (chromosome 19p13) causing non-atherosclerotic degeneration of small vessel smooth muscle cells; (2) **Migraine with aura** — often the earliest symptom, beginning in the 20s-30s; (3) **Recurrent ischemic strokes** — typically lacunar, beginning in the 30s-50s; (4) **Progressive cognitive decline** — subcortical vascular dementia with executive dysfunction; (5) **Psychiatric symptoms** — depression, apathy, pseudobulbar affect. MRI shows **extensive white matter hyperintensities** with **characteristic involvement of the anterior temporal poles and external capsules** (a finding highly suggestive of CADASIL). Diagnosis is confirmed by NOTCH3 gene testing or skin biopsy showing granular osmiophilic material (GOM) in vessel walls. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; Current D&T Geriatrics)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old woman is evaluated for a 4-year history of progressive cognitive impairment. The spouse reports that the patient''''s cognitive function varies dramatically from day to day, with episodes of extreme confusion alternating with near-normal lucidity. The patient has reported detailed **visual hallucinations** of small animals in the house. Examination reveals cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia, and a resting tremor. The spouse notes that the patient violently kicks and punches during sleep. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB) \u2014 characterized by the core features of fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, spontaneous parkinsonism, and REM sleep behavior disorder", "Alzheimer''''s disease with behavioral disturbance \u2014 visual hallucinations in AD typically occur only in the severe/end stage and are not a core diagnostic feature", "Parkinson''''s disease with psychosis \u2014 the motor symptoms must precede cognitive decline by at least 1 year to differentiate from DLB", "Delirium due to underlying infection \u2014 the fluctuating cognition and hallucinations suggest an acute metabolic or infectious etiology", "Schizophrenia with late onset \u2014 visual hallucinations in the elderly are pathognomonic for late-onset schizophrenia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient presents with all four **core clinical features of Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)**: (1) **Fluctuating cognition** — pronounced variations in attention and alertness, with episodes of confusion alternating with lucidity (''''good days and bad days''''); (2) **Recurrent visual hallucinations** — typically well-formed, detailed, and often non-threatening (people, animals, children); (3) **Spontaneous Parkinsonism** — rigidity, bradykinesia, resting tremor, postural instability (motor features are SPONTANEOUS, not drug-induced); (4) **REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD)** — loss of normal REM atonia, causing patients to physically act out dreams (kicking, punching, falling out of bed). DLB is the second most common neurodegenerative dementia after AD, accounting for 15-25% of cases. Histopathologically, it is characterized by **alpha-synuclein** inclusions (Lewy bodies) distributed throughout the cortex and brainstem. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK; McKeith Consensus Criteria 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old woman is evaluated for a 4-year history of progressive cognitive impairment. The spouse reports that the patient''''s cognitive function varies dramatically from day to day, with episodes of extreme confusion alternating with near-normal lucidity. The patient has reported detailed **visual hallucinations** of small animals in the house. Examination reveals cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia, and a resting tremor. The spouse notes that the patient violently kicks and punches during sleep. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Parkinson''''s disease with psychosis \u2014 the motor symptoms must precede cognitive decline by at least 1 year to differentiate from DLB", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB) \u2014 characterized by the core features of fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, spontaneous parkinsonism, and REM sleep behavior disorder", "Delirium due to underlying infection \u2014 the fluctuating cognition and hallucinations suggest an acute metabolic or infectious etiology", "Schizophrenia with late onset \u2014 visual hallucinations in the elderly are pathognomonic for late-onset schizophrenia", "Alzheimer''''s disease with behavioral disturbance \u2014 visual hallucinations in AD typically occur only in the severe/end stage and are not a core diagnostic feature"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient presents with all four **core clinical features of Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)**: (1) **Fluctuating cognition** — pronounced variations in attention and alertness, with episodes of confusion alternating with lucidity (''''good days and bad days''''); (2) **Recurrent visual hallucinations** — typically well-formed, detailed, and often non-threatening (people, animals, children); (3) **Spontaneous Parkinsonism** — rigidity, bradykinesia, resting tremor, postural instability (motor features are SPONTANEOUS, not drug-induced); (4) **REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD)** — loss of normal REM atonia, causing patients to physically act out dreams (kicking, punching, falling out of bed). DLB is the second most common neurodegenerative dementia after AD, accounting for 15-25% of cases. Histopathologically, it is characterized by **alpha-synuclein** inclusions (Lewy bodies) distributed throughout the cortex and brainstem. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK; McKeith Consensus Criteria 2017)'),
