    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old woman is evaluated for a 5-year history of progressive cognitive impairment. The spouse reports that the patient''''s cognitive function varies dramatically from day to day, with episodes of extreme confusion alternating with near-normal lucidity. The patient has reported detailed **visual hallucinations** of small animals in the house. Examination reveals cogwheel rigidity, bradykinesia, and a resting tremor. The spouse notes that the patient violently kicks and punches during sleep. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Schizophrenia with late onset \u2014 visual hallucinations in the elderly are pathognomonic for late-onset schizophrenia", "Parkinson''''s disease with psychosis \u2014 the motor symptoms must precede cognitive decline by at least 1 year to differentiate from DLB", "Delirium due to underlying infection \u2014 the fluctuating cognition and hallucinations suggest an acute metabolic or infectious etiology", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB) \u2014 characterized by the core features of fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, spontaneous parkinsonism, and REM sleep behavior disorder", "Alzheimer''''s disease with behavioral disturbance \u2014 visual hallucinations in AD typically occur only in the severe/end stage and are not a core diagnostic feature"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient presents with all four **core clinical features of Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)**: (1) **Fluctuating cognition** — pronounced variations in attention and alertness, with episodes of confusion alternating with lucidity (''''good days and bad days''''); (2) **Recurrent visual hallucinations** — typically well-formed, detailed, and often non-threatening (people, animals, children); (3) **Spontaneous Parkinsonism** — rigidity, bradykinesia, resting tremor, postural instability (motor features are SPONTANEOUS, not drug-induced); (4) **REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD)** — loss of normal REM atonia, causing patients to physically act out dreams (kicking, punching, falling out of bed). DLB is the second most common neurodegenerative dementia after AD, accounting for 15-25% of cases. Histopathologically, it is characterized by **alpha-synuclein** inclusions (Lewy bodies) distributed throughout the cortex and brainstem. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; First Aid Step 2 CK; McKeith Consensus Criteria 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old male with recently diagnosed Dementia with Lewy Bodies is admitted to the hospital for management of distressing visual hallucinations. The on-call physician, unaware of the diagnosis, administers **haloperidol 5 mg IM**. Within hours, the patient develops severe rigidity, high fever (40.1°C), autonomic instability (labile blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis), and obtundation. Which of the following best explains this life-threatening reaction?', '["Malignant hyperthermia triggered by the intramuscular injection technique rather than the medication itself", "Anticholinergic toxicity from haloperidol causing central hyperthermia, mydriasis, and urinary retention", "Serotonin syndrome caused by an interaction between haloperidol and the patient''''s cholinesterase inhibitor medication", "Neuroleptic sensitivity in DLB \u2014 patients with Lewy body disease have extreme sensitivity to dopamine D2 receptor blockade by antipsychotics, which can trigger severe extrapyramidal symptoms, neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS), irreversible parkinsonism, and death", "Sepsis from a hospital-acquired urinary tract infection coincidentally developing at the same time as the haloperidol administration"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a classic and potentially fatal example of **neuroleptic sensitivity** in DLB. Patients with Lewy body disease (both DLB and Parkinson''''s disease dementia) have an **extreme sensitivity to dopamine D2 receptor blockade** by antipsychotic medications. Administration of typical antipsychotics (haloperidol, chlorpromazine) or high-potency atypical antipsychotics can trigger: (1) **Severe extrapyramidal symptoms** — profound rigidity, akinesia; (2) **Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)** — hyperthermia, autonomic instability, altered consciousness, elevated CK; (3) **Irreversible parkinsonism**; (4) **Increased mortality** (2-3x). This occurs because the already depleted dopaminergic system in DLB cannot tolerate further D2 blockade. If antipsychotics are absolutely necessary, use only **low-dose quetiapine** (least D2 binding) or **pimavanserin** (selective 5-HT2A inverse agonist, FDA-approved for Parkinson''''s disease psychosis, no D2 activity). **NEVER use haloperidol in DLB**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Beers Criteria; McKeith Consensus Criteria)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old female with recently diagnosed Dementia with Lewy Bodies is admitted to the hospital for management of distressing visual hallucinations. The on-call physician, unaware of the diagnosis, administers **haloperidol 5 mg IM**. Within hours, the patient develops severe rigidity, high fever (40.1°C), autonomic instability (labile blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis), and obtundation. Which of the following best explains this life-threatening reaction?', '["Anticholinergic toxicity from haloperidol causing central hyperthermia, mydriasis, and urinary retention", "Malignant hyperthermia triggered by the intramuscular injection technique rather than the medication itself", "Sepsis from a hospital-acquired urinary tract infection coincidentally developing at the same time as the haloperidol administration", "Neuroleptic sensitivity in DLB \u2014 patients with Lewy body disease have extreme sensitivity to dopamine D2 receptor blockade by antipsychotics, which can trigger severe extrapyramidal symptoms, neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS), irreversible parkinsonism, and death", "Serotonin syndrome caused by an interaction between haloperidol and the patient''''s cholinesterase inhibitor medication"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a classic and potentially fatal example of **neuroleptic sensitivity** in DLB. Patients with Lewy body disease (both DLB and Parkinson''''s disease dementia) have an **extreme sensitivity to dopamine D2 receptor blockade** by antipsychotic medications. Administration of typical antipsychotics (haloperidol, chlorpromazine) or high-potency atypical antipsychotics can trigger: (1) **Severe extrapyramidal symptoms** — profound rigidity, akinesia; (2) **Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)** — hyperthermia, autonomic instability, altered consciousness, elevated CK; (3) **Irreversible parkinsonism**; (4) **Increased mortality** (2-3x). This occurs because the already depleted dopaminergic system in DLB cannot tolerate further D2 