    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female with recently diagnosed Dementia with Lewy Bodies is admitted to the hospital for management of distressing visual hallucinations. The on-call physician, unaware of the diagnosis, administers **haloperidol 5 mg IM**. Within hours, the patient develops severe rigidity, high fever (40.1°C), autonomic instability (labile blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis), and obtundation. Which of the following best explains this life-threatening reaction?', '["Sepsis from a hospital-acquired urinary tract infection coincidentally developing at the same time as the haloperidol administration", "Serotonin syndrome caused by an interaction between haloperidol and the patient''''s cholinesterase inhibitor medication", "Anticholinergic toxicity from haloperidol causing central hyperthermia, mydriasis, and urinary retention", "Neuroleptic sensitivity in DLB \u2014 patients with Lewy body disease have extreme sensitivity to dopamine D2 receptor blockade by antipsychotics, which can trigger severe extrapyramidal symptoms, neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS), irreversible parkinsonism, and death", "Malignant hyperthermia triggered by the intramuscular injection technique rather than the medication itself"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is a classic and potentially fatal example of **neuroleptic sensitivity** in DLB. Patients with Lewy body disease (both DLB and Parkinson''''s disease dementia) have an **extreme sensitivity to dopamine D2 receptor blockade** by antipsychotic medications. Administration of typical antipsychotics (haloperidol, chlorpromazine) or high-potency atypical antipsychotics can trigger: (1) **Severe extrapyramidal symptoms** — profound rigidity, akinesia; (2) **Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)** — hyperthermia, autonomic instability, altered consciousness, elevated CK; (3) **Irreversible parkinsonism**; (4) **Increased mortality** (2-3x). This occurs because the already depleted dopaminergic system in DLB cannot tolerate further D2 blockade. If antipsychotics are absolutely necessary, use only **low-dose quetiapine** (least D2 binding) or **pimavanserin** (selective 5-HT2A inverse agonist, FDA-approved for Parkinson''''s disease psychosis, no D2 activity). **NEVER use haloperidol in DLB**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Beers Criteria; McKeith Consensus Criteria)'),
