lockade. If antipsychotics are absolutely necessary, use only **low-dose quetiapine** (least D2 binding) or **pimavanserin** (selective 5-HT2A inverse agonist, FDA-approved for Parkinson''''s disease psychosis, no D2 activity). **NEVER use haloperidol in DLB**. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Beers Criteria; McKeith Consensus Criteria)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old woman was diagnosed with Parkinson''''s disease 9 years ago and has been managed with carbidopa-levodopa. Over the past year, the patient has developed progressive cognitive decline with impaired attention, executive dysfunction, and visual hallucinations. Which of the following BEST distinguishes this patient''''s condition from Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)?', '["The distinction is based on MRI findings: DLB shows cortical atrophy while PDD shows only brainstem atrophy", "DLB and PDD are genetically distinct diseases with completely different pathological substrates \u2014 DLB involves tau protein while PDD involves alpha-synuclein", "The distinction is based on the type of hallucinations: DLB causes visual hallucinations while PDD causes exclusively auditory hallucinations", "Parkinson''''s Disease Dementia (PDD) \u2014 defined by the ''''1-year rule'''': if well-established Parkinson''''s motor symptoms precede the onset of dementia by more than 1 year, the diagnosis is PDD; if dementia occurs before or within 1 year of parkinsonism, the diagnosis is DLB", "PDD is diagnosed only when motor symptoms fail to respond to levodopa, whereas DLB always has levodopa-responsive parkinsonism"]'::jsonb, 3, '**DLB and Parkinson''''s Disease Dementia (PDD)** are considered part of the same **Lewy body disease spectrum** — both are caused by **alpha-synuclein** aggregation (Lewy bodies). The clinical distinction is based on the **''''1-year rule''''**: (1) **DLB**: Dementia develops **before, simultaneously with, or within 1 year** of parkinsonism. Cognitive symptoms are the primary or co-equal presenting feature. (2) **PDD**: Well-established Parkinson''''s disease motor symptoms (tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia) precede the onset of dementia by **more than 1 year** (typically 10+ years after PD diagnosis). In this case, the patient had PD for 9 years before developing dementia, making PDD the correct diagnosis. Both DLB and PDD can cause fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and REM sleep behavior disorder. Both respond to cholinesterase inhibitors (rivastigmine has the best evidence). Both require avoidance of typical antipsychotics. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; McKeith Consensus Criteria 2017)'),
