    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old woman was diagnosed with Parkinson''''s disease 9 years ago and has been managed with carbidopa-levodopa. Over the past year, the patient has developed progressive cognitive decline with impaired attention, executive dysfunction, and visual hallucinations. Which of the following BEST distinguishes this patient''''s condition from Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)?', '["The distinction is based on the type of hallucinations: DLB causes visual hallucinations while PDD causes exclusively auditory hallucinations", "Parkinson''''s Disease Dementia (PDD) \u2014 defined by the ''''1-year rule'''': if well-established Parkinson''''s motor symptoms precede the onset of dementia by more than 1 year, the diagnosis is PDD; if dementia occurs before or within 1 year of parkinsonism, the diagnosis is DLB", "The distinction is based on MRI findings: DLB shows cortical atrophy while PDD shows only brainstem atrophy", "DLB and PDD are genetically distinct diseases with completely different pathological substrates \u2014 DLB involves tau protein while PDD involves alpha-synuclein", "PDD is diagnosed only when motor symptoms fail to respond to levodopa, whereas DLB always has levodopa-responsive parkinsonism"]'::jsonb, 1, '**DLB and Parkinson''''s Disease Dementia (PDD)** are considered part of the same **Lewy body disease spectrum** — both are caused by **alpha-synuclein** aggregation (Lewy bodies). The clinical distinction is based on the **''''1-year rule''''**: (1) **DLB**: Dementia develops **before, simultaneously with, or within 1 year** of parkinsonism. Cognitive symptoms are the primary or co-equal presenting feature. (2) **PDD**: Well-established Parkinson''''s disease motor symptoms (tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia) precede the onset of dementia by **more than 1 year** (typically 10+ years after PD diagnosis). In this case, the patient had PD for 9 years before developing dementia, making PDD the correct diagnosis. Both DLB and PDD can cause fluctuating cognition, visual hallucinations, and REM sleep behavior disorder. Both respond to cholinesterase inhibitors (rivastigmine has the best evidence). Both require avoidance of typical antipsychotics. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; McKeith Consensus Criteria 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old man presents with a 4-year history of fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, and mild parkinsonian features. The clinical team suspects Dementia with Lewy Bodies but wants to confirm the diagnosis with objective testing. Which of the following diagnostic investigations is MOST helpful as a supportive biomarker for DLB?', '["Brain MRI showing bilateral hippocampal atrophy \u2014 this finding is specific for DLB and not seen in Alzheimer''''s disease", "EEG showing generalized 3 Hz spike-and-wave complexes \u2014 this pattern is pathognomonic for DLB", "Serum alpha-synuclein antibody levels \u2014 a commercially available blood test that definitively diagnoses DLB in all cases", "DaTSCAN (ioflupane I-123 SPECT) showing reduced dopamine transporter uptake in the caudate nucleus and putamen, confirming presynaptic dopaminergic degeneration (an indicative biomarker for DLB)", "Lumbar puncture showing elevated CSF amyloid-beta 42 \u2014 this is the gold standard biomarker for Lewy body pathology"]'::jsonb, 3, '**DaTSCAN** (ioflupane I-123 SPECT imaging) is an **indicative biomarker** for DLB per the 2017 McKeith Consensus Criteria. It measures **dopamine transporter (DAT) density** in the striatum (caudate and putamen). In DLB, there is **reduced DAT uptake** reflecting presynaptic dopaminergic neuronal loss — a finding shared with Parkinson''''s disease but NOT typically seen in Alzheimer''''s disease. This makes DaTSCAN particularly useful for differentiating DLB from AD when the clinical picture is ambiguous. Other supportive biomarkers include: (1) **Polysomnography (PSG)** confirming REM sleep without atonia (RBD); (2) **MIBG myocardial scintigraphy** showing reduced cardiac sympathetic innervation; (3) **FDG-PET** showing occipital hypometabolism with preserved posterior cingulate (''''cingulate island sign''''). Hippocampal atrophy is more typical of AD than DLB. CSF Aβ42 is an AD biomarker. (Ref: McKeith et al. 2017 Consensus Criteria; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
