 Consensus Criteria; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female presents with a 4-year history of fluctuating cognition, recurrent visual hallucinations, and mild parkinsonian features. The clinical team suspects Dementia with Lewy Bodies but wants to confirm the diagnosis with objective testing. Which of the following diagnostic investigations is MOST helpful as a supportive biomarker for DLB?', '["EEG showing generalized 3 Hz spike-and-wave complexes \u2014 this pattern is pathognomonic for DLB", "Brain MRI showing bilateral hippocampal atrophy \u2014 this finding is specific for DLB and not seen in Alzheimer''''s disease", "Serum alpha-synuclein antibody levels \u2014 a commercially available blood test that definitively diagnoses DLB in all cases", "Lumbar puncture showing elevated CSF amyloid-beta 42 \u2014 this is the gold standard biomarker for Lewy body pathology", "DaTSCAN (ioflupane I-123 SPECT) showing reduced dopamine transporter uptake in the caudate nucleus and putamen, confirming presynaptic dopaminergic degeneration (an indicative biomarker for DLB)"]'::jsonb, 4, '**DaTSCAN** (ioflupane I-123 SPECT imaging) is an **indicative biomarker** for DLB per the 2017 McKeith Consensus Criteria. It measures **dopamine transporter (DAT) density** in the striatum (caudate and putamen). In DLB, there is **reduced DAT uptake** reflecting presynaptic dopaminergic neuronal loss — a finding shared with Parkinson''''s disease but NOT typically seen in Alzheimer''''s disease. This makes DaTSCAN particularly useful for differentiating DLB from AD when the clinical picture is ambiguous. Other supportive biomarkers include: (1) **Polysomnography (PSG)** confirming REM sleep without atonia (RBD); (2) **MIBG myocardial scintigraphy** showing reduced cardiac sympathetic innervation; (3) **FDG-PET** showing occipital hypometabolism with preserved posterior cingulate (''''cingulate island sign''''). Hippocampal atrophy is more typical of AD than DLB. CSF Aβ42 is an AD biomarker. (Ref: McKeith et al. 2017 Consensus Criteria; Hazzard''''s Ch. 72)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old man with known Dementia with Lewy Bodies presents with recurrent near-syncope when standing up from a seated position, severe constipation (bowel movements every 5-7 days), and new-onset urinary incontinence. Orthostatic vitals show a systolic blood pressure drop of **35 mmHg** upon standing with a heart rate increase of only 5 bpm. Which of the following best explains this constellation of symptoms?', '["Congestive heart failure with low cardiac output \u2014 the orthostatic changes indicate severely reduced ejection fraction requiring urgent echocardiography", "Diabetic autonomic neuropathy \u2014 this diagnosis is most likely even without a history of diabetes mellitus", "Autonomic dysfunction due to alpha-synuclein deposition in peripheral autonomic ganglia and brainstem autonomic nuclei, causing orthostatic hypotension, constipation, urinary dysfunction, and impaired heart rate response \u2014 a common and often underrecognized feature of Lewy body disease", "Dehydration due to inadequate oral intake \u2014 fluid replacement alone will completely resolve all symptoms including constipation and urinary incontinence", "Medication-induced orthostatic hypotension from cholinesterase inhibitors alone \u2014 this resolves immediately upon drug discontinuation"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Autonomic dysfunction** is a common and often underrecognized feature of **Lewy body disease** (both DLB and PDD). Alpha-synuclein deposits accumulate in **peripheral autonomic ganglia**, the **intermediolateral cell column** of the spinal cord, and **brainstem autonomic nuclei** (dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus), causing: (1) **Orthostatic hypotension** — systolic drop ≥20 mmHg or diastolic drop ≥10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing, with blunted heart rate response (neurogenic OH); (2) **Constipation** — due to reduced GI motility from vagal denervation; (3) **Urinary dysfunction** — urgency, frequency, incontinence; (4) **Excessive sweating or anhidrosis**; (5) **Sialorrhea** (drooling). Management of orthostatic hypotension includes: compression stockings, increased salt/fluid intake, elevating head of bed, and if needed, **midodrine** or **droxidopa** (FDA-approved for neurogenic OH). Fludrocortisone is second-line. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
