    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old man with known Dementia with Lewy Bodies presents with recurrent near-syncope when standing up from a seated position, severe constipation (bowel movements every 5-7 days), and new-onset urinary incontinence. Orthostatic vitals show a systolic blood pressure drop of **35 mmHg** upon standing with a heart rate increase of only 5 bpm. Which of the following best explains this constellation of symptoms?', '["Diabetic autonomic neuropathy \u2014 this diagnosis is most likely even without a history of diabetes mellitus", "Congestive heart failure with low cardiac output \u2014 the orthostatic changes indicate severely reduced ejection fraction requiring urgent echocardiography", "Medication-induced orthostatic hypotension from cholinesterase inhibitors alone \u2014 this resolves immediately upon drug discontinuation", "Autonomic dysfunction due to alpha-synuclein deposition in peripheral autonomic ganglia and brainstem autonomic nuclei, causing orthostatic hypotension, constipation, urinary dysfunction, and impaired heart rate response \u2014 a common and often underrecognized feature of Lewy body disease", "Dehydration due to inadequate oral intake \u2014 fluid replacement alone will completely resolve all symptoms including constipation and urinary incontinence"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Autonomic dysfunction** is a common and often underrecognized feature of **Lewy body disease** (both DLB and PDD). Alpha-synuclein deposits accumulate in **peripheral autonomic ganglia**, the **intermediolateral cell column** of the spinal cord, and **brainstem autonomic nuclei** (dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus), causing: (1) **Orthostatic hypotension** — systolic drop ≥20 mmHg or diastolic drop ≥10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing, with blunted heart rate response (neurogenic OH); (2) **Constipation** — due to reduced GI motility from vagal denervation; (3) **Urinary dysfunction** — urgency, frequency, incontinence; (4) **Excessive sweating or anhidrosis**; (5) **Sialorrhea** (drooling). Management of orthostatic hypotension includes: compression stockings, increased salt/fluid intake, elevating head of bed, and if needed, **midodrine** or **droxidopa** (FDA-approved for neurogenic OH). Fludrocortisone is second-line. (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old male with Dementia with Lewy Bodies presents with worsening cognitive function, visual hallucinations that are now causing significant distress, and mild parkinsonian symptoms (bradykinesia and rigidity but no tremor). Which of the following represents the most appropriate pharmacological management plan?', '["No pharmacotherapy is indicated for DLB; all treatment is non-pharmacological including environmental modifications and behavioral therapy only", "Rivastigmine (cholinesterase inhibitor) as first-line for both cognitive and behavioral symptoms; low-dose carbidopa-levodopa for motor symptoms if functionally limiting; pimavanserin or low-dose quetiapine if hallucinations are distressing and unresponsive to cholinesterase inhibitor therapy", "High-dose haloperidol for hallucinations, benztropine for rigidity, and memantine for cognitive symptoms", "Donepezil for cognition, high-dose trihexyphenidyl (anticholinergic) for motor symptoms, and olanzapine for hallucinations", "High-dose dopamine agonists (pramipexole 4.5 mg daily) as first-line for all DLB symptoms including cognition, hallucinations, and motor features"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The pharmacological management of DLB requires a careful, balanced approach because treating one symptom domain can worsen another: **Cognitive/behavioral symptoms**: **Cholinesterase inhibitors** are first-line — **rivastigmine** has the strongest evidence for DLB (significant benefits for attention, hallucinations, and behavioral symptoms). **Motor symptoms**: **Low-dose carbidopa-levodopa** is preferred over dopamine agonists (agonists are more likely to worsen hallucinations and psychosis). Start at low doses and titrate slowly. **Hallucinations**: If non-distressing, reassurance and observation. If distressing, first optimize the cholinesterase inhibitor. If still problematic, use **pimavanserin** (5-HT2A inverse agonist, no D2 activity) or **low-dose quetiapine** (lowest D2 affinity among atypical antipsychotics). **NEVER USE**: haloperidol, olanzapine, risperidone (neuroleptic sensitivity), anticholinergics like benztropine/trihexyphenidyl (worsen cognition, cause delirium), or high-dose dopamine agonists (worsen psychosis). (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; McKeith Consensus 2017; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
