    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old man with Dementia with Lewy Bodies presents with worsening cognitive function, visual hallucinations that are now causing significant distress, and mild parkinsonian symptoms (bradykinesia and rigidity but no tremor). Which of the following represents the most appropriate pharmacological management plan?', '["High-dose dopamine agonists (pramipexole 4.5 mg daily) as first-line for all DLB symptoms including cognition, hallucinations, and motor features", "Donepezil for cognition, high-dose trihexyphenidyl (anticholinergic) for motor symptoms, and olanzapine for hallucinations", "Rivastigmine (cholinesterase inhibitor) as first-line for both cognitive and behavioral symptoms; low-dose carbidopa-levodopa for motor symptoms if functionally limiting; pimavanserin or low-dose quetiapine if hallucinations are distressing and unresponsive to cholinesterase inhibitor therapy", "No pharmacotherapy is indicated for DLB; all treatment is non-pharmacological including environmental modifications and behavioral therapy only", "High-dose haloperidol for hallucinations, benztropine for rigidity, and memantine for cognitive symptoms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pharmacological management of DLB requires a careful, balanced approach because treating one symptom domain can worsen another: **Cognitive/behavioral symptoms**: **Cholinesterase inhibitors** are first-line — **rivastigmine** has the strongest evidence for DLB (significant benefits for attention, hallucinations, and behavioral symptoms). **Motor symptoms**: **Low-dose carbidopa-levodopa** is preferred over dopamine agonists (agonists are more likely to worsen hallucinations and psychosis). Start at low doses and titrate slowly. **Hallucinations**: If non-distressing, reassurance and observation. If distressing, first optimize the cholinesterase inhibitor. If still problematic, use **pimavanserin** (5-HT2A inverse agonist, no D2 activity) or **low-dose quetiapine** (lowest D2 affinity among atypical antipsychotics). **NEVER USE**: haloperidol, olanzapine, risperidone (neuroleptic sensitivity), anticholinergics like benztropine/trihexyphenidyl (worsen cognition, cause delirium), or high-dose dopamine agonists (worsen psychosis). (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; McKeith Consensus 2017; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female with Dementia with Lewy Bodies presents with worsening cognitive function, visual hallucinations that are now causing significant distress, and mild parkinsonian symptoms (bradykinesia and rigidity but no tremor). Which of the following represents the most appropriate pharmacological management plan?', '["Rivastigmine (cholinesterase inhibitor) as first-line for both cognitive and behavioral symptoms; low-dose carbidopa-levodopa for motor symptoms if functionally limiting; pimavanserin or low-dose quetiapine if hallucinations are distressing and unresponsive to cholinesterase inhibitor therapy", "High-dose haloperidol for hallucinations, benztropine for rigidity, and memantine for cognitive symptoms", "High-dose dopamine agonists (pramipexole 4.5 mg daily) as first-line for all DLB symptoms including cognition, hallucinations, and motor features", "No pharmacotherapy is indicated for DLB; all treatment is non-pharmacological including environmental modifications and behavioral therapy only", "Donepezil for cognition, high-dose trihexyphenidyl (anticholinergic) for motor symptoms, and olanzapine for hallucinations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pharmacological management of DLB requires a careful, balanced approach because treating one symptom domain can worsen another: **Cognitive/behavioral symptoms**: **Cholinesterase inhibitors** are first-line — **rivastigmine** has the strongest evidence for DLB (significant benefits for attention, hallucinations, and behavioral symptoms). **Motor symptoms**: **Low-dose carbidopa-levodopa** is preferred over dopamine agonists (agonists are more likely to worsen hallucinations and psychosis). Start at low doses and titrate slowly. **Hallucinations**: If non-distressing, reassurance and observation. If distressing, first optimize the cholinesterase inhibitor. If still problematic, use **pimavanserin** (5-HT2A inverse agonist, no D2 activity) or **low-dose quetiapine** (lowest D2 affinity among atypical antipsychotics). **NEVER USE**: haloperidol, olanzapine, risperidone (neuroleptic sensitivity), anticholinergics like benztropine/trihexyphenidyl (worsen cognition, cause delirium), or high-dose dopamine agonists (worsen psychosis). (Ref: Hazzard''''s Ch. 72; McKeith Consensus 2017; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)');
